use std
into lzma.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace lzma). It decodes LZMA data held in memory: raw LZMA streams, LZMA2 chunk streams, the .xz container (several streams one after another, stream padding, block checks with CRC-32, CRC-64 and SHA-256, the LZMA2, Delta and BCJ x86 filters) and the legacy .lzma format ("alone"). Every module-level name starts with `lz_` or `LZ_`. Problems are reported with `lz_warn`. The output list is also the dictionary: a match may reach back to where the current dictionary began in the output, never further. The dictionary size written in headers is read but not enforced.

```amni
import dm;
import pack;
import crypto.hash;
```

Other modules used: `pack::pk_crc32(s const byte[:], crc int) int` continues a CRC-32 over s (call it with crc 0 to start; the result is from 0 to 4294967295), `dm::dm_warn_add(msg const byte[:])` adds one warning line and `crypto::sha256(d const byte[:]) byte[>..]` returns the 32-byte SHA-256 digest of d.

`var lz_cap = 268435456` is the most bytes one decompression may produce in total (256 MiB); the tests lower it. `var lz_capped = false` is set when that limit was hit and `var lz_said = false` when a warning was added since the current top-level call started. `lz_warn(msg const byte[:])` sets `lz_said` to true and calls `dm::dm_warn_add(msg)`. `lz_over() bool` sets `lz_capped` to true, calls `lz_warn("decompressed data passes the limit of 256 MiB")` and returns false.

`lz_le(s const byte[:], p int, n int) int` reads an unsigned little-endian number of n bytes (1 to 8) at offset p of s, building it in an int one byte at a time from the highest byte down. It returns -1 when p is negative, when p plus n passes the end of s, or when an 8-byte value is 2 to the power 63 or more.

`var lz_c64 u64[>..]` is the CRC-64 table, 256 entries built once by `lz_crc_init()` when it is empty: entry n starts as n (as a u64) and eight times becomes (c shifted right by one) xor 0xC96C5795D7870F42 when its lowest bit is set and c shifted right by one when not. `lz_crc64(s const byte[:], crc u64) u64` continues the xz CRC-64 over s: it calls `lz_crc_init()`, starts with c equal to crc xor 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, for every byte b sets c to `lz_c64` at index (c xor b) and 255, xor (c shifted right by 8), and returns c xor 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF; call it with crc 0 to start. `lz_le64(s const byte[:], p int) u64` reads 8 bytes little-endian at p as a u64 (the caller guarantees they exist).

Range coder state: `var lz_pos = 0` (the next input byte), `var lz_lim = 0` (the input offset where reading must stop), `var lz_rng = 0`, `var lz_code = 0` and `var lz_bad = false`; all stay below 2 to the power 32. The probabilities are `var lz_pr int[>..]`: each is the chance of a zero bit out of 2048, 1024 when fresh. Its layout is given by these module `let` values: `LZ_ISREP` 192, `LZ_REPG0` 204, `LZ_REPG1` 216, `LZ_REPG2` 228, `LZ_REP0L` 240, `LZ_SLOT` 432, `LZ_SPEC` 688, `LZ_ALIGN` 803, `LZ_LEN` 819, `LZ_RLEN` 1333 and `LZ_LIT` 1847. Entries 0 to 191 are the is-match bits (index: state times 16 plus position state); `LZ_ISREP`, `LZ_REPG0`, `LZ_REPG1` and `LZ_REPG2` hold 12 entries each (index: state); `LZ_REP0L` holds 192 (state times 16 plus position state); `LZ_SLOT` holds four position slot trees of 64 entries (length state times 64 plus the tree index); `LZ_SPEC` holds 115 entries; `LZ_ALIGN` holds 16; `LZ_LEN` and `LZ_RLEN` each hold a length coder of 514 entries (entry 0 the first choice bit, entry 1 the second choice bit, entries 2 to 129 sixteen low trees of 8 entries each (position state times 8 plus the tree index), entries 130 to 257 sixteen mid trees of 8, entries 258 to 513 the high tree of 256); from `LZ_LIT` on there are 768 entries for each literal context.

Decoder state: `var lz_lc = 0`, `var lz_lp = 0`, `var lz_pb = 0` (the literal context bits, literal position bits and position bits), `var lz_st = 0` (the state, 0 to 11), `var lz_r0 = 0`, `var lz_r1 = 0`, `var lz_r2 = 0`, `var lz_r3 = 0` (the four most recent match distances, each stored as the distance minus 1) and `var lz_dst = 0` (the index in the output list where the current dictionary starts; the position of a byte is its index minus `lz_dst`). `var lz_dict = 4294967295` is the dictionary size: no match distance (stored minus 1) may reach it. `var lz_used = 0` is how many input bytes the last successful `lz_lzma_dec` or `lz_lzma2_dec` call consumed. `lz_dsize(prop int) int` is the LZMA2 dictionary size for a properties byte: 4294967295 when prop is 40, otherwise (2 or (prop and 1)) shifted left by ((prop shifted right by 1) plus 11).

`lz_props(b int) bool` splits an LZMA properties byte: it returns false when b is 225 or more; otherwise it sets `lz_lc` to b modulo 9, `lz_lp` to (b divided by 9) modulo 5 and `lz_pb` to b divided by 45, and returns true.

`lz_reset(lc int, lp int, pb int)` stores lc, lp and pb in `lz_lc`, `lz_lp`, `lz_pb`, clears `lz_pr` and pushes 1024 until it holds `LZ_LIT` plus 768 shifted left by lc plus lp entries, and sets `lz_st`, `lz_r0`, `lz_r1`, `lz_r2` and `lz_r3` to 0.

`lz_rc_init(src const byte[:], p int, lim int) bool` starts the range coder at offset p of src: it sets `lz_pos` to p plus 5, `lz_lim` to lim, `lz_bad` to false and `lz_rng` to 0xFFFFFFFF, and `lz_code` to the 4 bytes at p plus 1 read big-endian. It returns false when p plus 5 is more than lim or more than the length of src, when the byte at p is not 0, or when `lz_code` equals `lz_rng`.

`lz_norm(src const byte[:])` normalizes the range coder: when `lz_rng` is below 16777216, `lz_rng` becomes (`lz_rng` shifted left by 8) and 0xFFFFFFFF and `lz_code` becomes ((`lz_code` shifted left by 8) or the next input byte) and 0xFFFFFFFF. The next input byte is src at `lz_pos` followed by adding 1 to `lz_pos` while `lz_pos` is below `lz_lim` (and below the length of src); otherwise it is 0 and `lz_bad` is set to true.

`lz_bit(src const byte[:], i int) int` decodes one bit with the probability `lz_pr` at index i: p is that entry and bound is (`lz_rng` shifted right by 11) times p. When `lz_code` is below bound: `lz_rng` becomes bound, the entry becomes p plus ((2048 minus p) shifted right by 5) and the bit is 0. Otherwise `lz_rng` and `lz_code` both drop by bound, the entry becomes p minus (p shifted right by 5) and the bit is 1. Then it calls `lz_norm(src)` and returns the bit.

`lz_tree(src const byte[:], base int, n int) int` decodes an n-bit number most significant bit first with a bit tree whose probabilities start at index base: m starts at 1 and n times becomes (m shifted left by 1) or `lz_bit(src, base plus m)`; the result is m minus (1 shifted left by n). `lz_rtree(src const byte[:], base int, n int) int` decodes an n-bit number least significant bit first the same way: m starts at 1 and the result at 0; for i from 0 to n minus 1 the bit is `lz_bit(src, base plus m)`, m becomes (m shifted left by 1) or the bit and the result gets the bit shifted left by i or-ed in. `lz_direct(src const byte[:], n int) int` decodes n bits without probabilities, most significant first: the result starts at 0 and n times: `lz_rng` becomes `lz_rng` shifted right by 1; when `lz_code` is at least `lz_rng` then `lz_code` drops by `lz_rng` and the bit is 1, otherwise the bit is 0; call `lz_norm(src)`; the result becomes (result shifted left by 1) or the bit.

`lz_len(src const byte[:], base int, ps int) int` decodes a match length minus 2 (0 to 271) with the length coder that starts at index base (`LZ_LEN` or `LZ_RLEN`) and position state ps: when `lz_bit(src, base)` is 0 the result is `lz_tree(src, base plus 2 plus ps times 8, 3)`; otherwise when `lz_bit(src, base plus 1)` is 0 it is 8 plus `lz_tree(src, base plus 130 plus ps times 8, 3)`; otherwise it is 16 plus `lz_tree(src, base plus 258, 8)`.

`lz_dist(src const byte[:], len int) int` decodes a match distance minus 1, where len is the match length minus 2: ls is the smaller of len and 3; slot is `lz_tree(src, LZ_SLOT plus ls times 64, 6)`; when slot is below 4 the result is slot. Otherwise nb is (slot shifted right by 1) minus 1 and d is (2 or (slot and 1)) shifted left by nb. When slot is below 14 the result is d plus `lz_rtree(src, LZ_SPEC plus d minus slot, nb)`. Otherwise the result is d plus (`lz_direct(src, nb minus 4)` shifted left by 4) plus `lz_rtree(src, LZ_ALIGN, 4)`. The value 4294967295 means the end marker.

`lz_lit(src const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` decodes one literal and pushes it onto out. n is out's length minus `lz_dst` and prev is 0 when n is 0 and the last byte of out otherwise. The coder base b is `LZ_LIT` plus 768 times (((n and (1 shifted left by `lz_lp` minus 1)) shifted left by `lz_lc`) plus (prev shifted right by (8 minus `lz_lc`))). sym starts at 1. When `lz_st` is 7 or more, mb is the byte of out at index out's length minus `lz_r0` minus 1 (set `lz_bad` and return when `lz_r0` is not below n), and the match-byte loop runs: mbit is (mb shifted right by 7) and 1, mb becomes (mb shifted left by 1) and 255, bit is `lz_bit(src, b plus ((1 plus mbit) shifted left by 8) plus sym)`, sym becomes (sym shifted left by 1) or bit, and the loop continues only while sym is below 256 and mbit equals bit. Then while sym is below 256, sym becomes (sym shifted left by 1) or `lz_bit(src, b plus sym)`. Push sym minus 256 as a byte. Finally `lz_st` becomes 0 when it is below 4, `lz_st` minus 3 when it is below 10 and `lz_st` minus 6 otherwise.

`lz_match(src const byte[:], out byte[>..]&, ps int, rem int) int` handles a match after the is-match bit was 1; rem is how many more output bytes are allowed, n is out's length minus `lz_dst`. It returns 0 when a match was copied, 1 for the end marker and -1 for failure. The first bit is `lz_bit` at index `LZ_ISREP` plus `lz_st`. When it is 0 (a new match): `lz_r3` becomes `lz_r2`, `lz_r2` becomes `lz_r1`, `lz_r1` becomes `lz_r0`, len is `lz_len` with the coder `LZ_LEN` and position state ps, `lz_st` becomes 7 when it is below 7 and 10 otherwise, and `lz_r0` becomes `lz_dist` of len; when `lz_r0` is 4294967295 return 1. When it is 1 (a repeated match): fail when n is 0. The next bit is `lz_bit` at index `LZ_REPG0` plus `lz_st`. When that bit is 0 the following bit is `lz_bit` at index `LZ_REP0L` plus (`lz_st` shifted left by 4) plus ps; when that one is 0 too it is a short repeat of one byte: `lz_st` becomes 9 when it is below 7 and 11 otherwise, fail when `lz_bad` is set or `lz_r0` is not below n or not below `lz_dict`, fail through `lz_over()` when out already holds `lz_cap` bytes, push the byte of out at index out's length minus `lz_r0` minus 1 and return 0; when that bit is 1 the distances stay as they are. When the REPG0 bit is 1 a distance d is chosen: when `lz_bit` at index `LZ_REPG1` plus `lz_st` is 0, d is `lz_r1`; otherwise when `lz_bit` at index `LZ_REPG2` plus `lz_st` is 0, d is `lz_r2`, and otherwise d is `lz_r3` and `lz_r3` becomes `lz_r2`; in the last two cases `lz_r2` becomes `lz_r1`; then `lz_r1` becomes `lz_r0` and `lz_r0` becomes d. Then (long repeat) len is `lz_len` with the coder `LZ_RLEN` and position state ps, and `lz_st` becomes 8 when it is below 7 and 11 otherwise. For both kinds of match the length is len plus 2. Fail when `lz_bad` is set, when `lz_r0` is not below n or not below `lz_dict`, or when the length is more than rem; fail through `lz_over()` when out's length plus the length would pass `lz_cap`; then push that many bytes one at a time, each one copied from the distance `lz_r0` plus 1 back (the copy may overlap), and return 0.

`lz_run(src const byte[:], out byte[>..]&, size int) bool` decodes symbols and appends bytes to out until size bytes were appended (size 0 or more) or until the end marker (size negative), using the state already set up by `lz_reset` and `lz_rc_init`. base is out's length at the start. First, when size is 0 or more and base plus size is more than `lz_cap`, return `lz_over()`. Then loop: done is out's length minus base; when size is 0 or more and done equals size, return true. ps is (out's length minus `lz_dst`) and (1 shifted left by `lz_pb`, minus 1). The is-match bit is `lz_bit` at index (`lz_st` shifted left by 4) plus ps. When it is 0 the symbol is a literal: when out already holds `lz_cap` bytes return `lz_over()`, otherwise call `lz_lit(src, out)`. When it is 1 call `lz_match(src, out, ps, rem)` where rem is size minus done when size is 0 or more and 1099511627776 otherwise; a result of 1 (end marker) returns true only when size is negative and `lz_bad` is false and `lz_code` is 0, and false otherwise; a negative result returns false. After every symbol return false when `lz_bad` is set.

`lz_lzma_dec(src const byte[:], props const byte[:], size int, out byte[>..]&) bool` decodes one raw LZMA stream (no header; the whole of src is the range coded data) and appends it to out. props is 5 bytes: the properties byte (`lz_props`) and the 4-byte little-endian dictionary size (`lz_dict` becomes it, but at least 4096); size is the number of bytes to produce, negative meaning until the end marker. It sets `lz_capped` and `lz_said` to false, remembers n, out's length at the start, sets `lz_dst` to n, sets `lz_dict`, calls `lz_reset` with the decoded lc, lp, pb, then `lz_rc_init(src, 0, src.len)` and `lz_run(src, out, size)`. When size is 0 or more and `lz_run` succeeded but `lz_code` is not 0, an end marker must follow the declared size: ps is (out's length minus `lz_dst`) and (1 shifted left by `lz_pb`, minus 1), the is-match bit `lz_bit` at index (`lz_st` shifted left by 4) plus ps must be 1, `lz_match(src, out, ps, 1)` must return 1, and afterwards `lz_bad` must be false and `lz_code` must be 0; when any of that does not hold it counts as a failed step. On success `lz_used` becomes `lz_pos`. It fails (truncating out back to n, and when `lz_capped` is false adding the warning "lzma: corrupt or truncated data" through `lz_warn`) when props is shorter than 5 bytes, `lz_props` fails, or any step fails.

`lz_lzma2_run(src const byte[:], start int, eof_ok bool, out byte[>..]&, endp int&) bool` decodes an LZMA2 chunk stream that begins at offset start of src and appends to out; it stops after the end byte 0 and sets endp to the offset after it, and returns true. When the input ends exactly between chunks it returns true (endp is the length of src) only if eof_ok is true, otherwise false. Two flags start true: need_dict and need_props. Each chunk begins with a control byte c at offset p. c equal to 0 ends the stream. When c is 224 or more, or equals 1, the dictionary is reset: `lz_dst` becomes out's length, need_dict becomes false and need_props becomes true; otherwise fail when need_dict is true. When c is 128 or more it is an LZMA chunk: fail when p plus 5 passes the length of src; usize is ((c and 31) shifted left by 16, or-ed with the 2 bytes at p plus 1 read big-endian) plus 1; csize is the 2 bytes at p plus 3 read big-endian, plus 1; q is p plus 5. When c is at least 192 the chunk carries new properties: the properties byte is the byte at q (fail when q is at the end of src), q grows by 1, fail when `lz_props` fails for it or `lz_lc` plus `lz_lp` is more than 4, need_props becomes false and `lz_reset` is called with `lz_lc`, `lz_lp`, `lz_pb` (a state reset with the new properties). Otherwise fail when need_props is true; otherwise when c is at least 160 call `lz_reset` with `lz_lc`, `lz_lp`, `lz_pb` (state reset only; below 160 nothing is reset). Then fail when q plus csize passes the length of src, or `lz_rc_init(src, q, q plus csize)` fails, or `lz_run(src, out, usize)` fails, or afterwards `lz_pos` is not q plus csize or `lz_code` is not 0; the next chunk starts at q plus csize. When c is 1 or 2 it is an uncompressed chunk: fail when p plus 3 passes the length of src; usize is the 2 bytes at p plus 1 read big-endian, plus 1; the data are the usize bytes at p plus 3 (fail when they pass the end of src; fail through `lz_over()` when out's length plus usize would pass `lz_cap`); append them and the next chunk starts right after them. Any other c (3 to 127) fails.

`lz_lzma2_dec(src const byte[:], prop int, out byte[>..]&) bool` decodes a complete LZMA2 stream (as 7z stores it) from the start of src and appends it to out. prop is the dictionary size byte of the coder's properties, which must be 40 or less. It sets `lz_capped` and `lz_said` to false, remembers n, out's length, sets `lz_dict` to `lz_dsize(prop)`, and calls `lz_lzma2_run(src, 0, true, out, e)`; on success `lz_used` becomes e; on any failure it truncates out back to n and, when `lz_capped` is false, adds "lzma2: corrupt or truncated data" through `lz_warn`, and returns false.

`lz_x86(buf byte[>..]&, start int, ip int)` undoes the BCJ x86 filter in place on the bytes of buf from index start to its end, exactly as the x86 decoder of xz does when it is given all the data at once, with ip the position of the first byte (0 normally). All 32-bit arithmetic is done in an int and masked with 0xFFFFFFFF after every addition or subtraction. n is buf's length minus start; do nothing when n is less than 5. `Test86(b)` means b is 0 or 255. `prev_mask` starts at 0 and `prev_pos` at 4294967291 (minus 5 as a 32-bit number); when (ip minus `prev_pos`) and 0xFFFFFFFF is more than 5 then `prev_pos` becomes (ip minus 5) and 0xFFFFFFFF. limit is n minus 5 and i starts at 0. While i is at most limit: b is the byte of buf at start plus i; when b is neither 0xE8 nor 0xE9, i grows by 1 and the loop continues. Otherwise offset is (ip plus i minus `prev_pos`) and 0xFFFFFFFF and `prev_pos` becomes (ip plus i) and 0xFFFFFFFF; when offset is more than 5, `prev_mask` becomes 0, otherwise, repeated offset times, `prev_mask` becomes (`prev_mask` and 0x77) shifted left by 1. Then b becomes the byte at start plus i plus 4. When Test86(b) holds, the allowed table at index (`prev_mask` shifted right by 1) and 7 is true (the allowed table for indices 0 to 7 is true, true, true, false, true, false, false, false) and `prev_mask` shifted right by 1 is below 16: src is the 32-bit little-endian number made of the bytes at start plus i plus 1 to start plus i plus 4; then loop: dest is (src minus (ip plus i plus 5)) and 0xFFFFFFFF; when `prev_mask` is 0 leave the loop; k is the bit-number table at index `prev_mask` shifted right by 1 (the table for indices 0 to 7 is 0, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3); b becomes (dest shifted right by (24 minus k times 8)) and 255; when not Test86(b) leave the loop; otherwise src becomes dest xor ((1 shifted left by (32 minus k times 8)) minus 1) and the loop repeats. After the loop the byte at start plus i plus 4 becomes 255 when bit 24 of dest is set and 0 otherwise, the bytes at start plus i plus 3, plus 2 and plus 1 become (dest shifted right by 16), (dest shifted right by 8) and dest, each and 255, i grows by 5 and `prev_mask` becomes 0. When the test failed, i grows by 1, `prev_mask` becomes `prev_mask` or 1, and when Test86(b) holds `prev_mask` becomes `prev_mask` or 16.

`lz_delta(buf byte[>..]&, start int, dist int)` undoes the Delta filter in place on the bytes of buf from index start to its end: for every index j from start plus dist up, the byte becomes (itself plus the byte at j minus dist) and 255 (the bytes at j minus dist are already decoded, so go upward).

`lz_is_xz(d const byte[:]) bool` is true when d has at least 12 bytes and starts with the six bytes 0xFD, 0x37, 0x7A, 0x58, 0x5A, 0x00.

`lz_varint(d const byte[:], p int&, lim int) int` reads an xz variable-length number at p and moves p past it: each byte gives its low 7 bits, shifted left by 7 times its position (least significant group first), and a set top bit means another byte follows. It returns -1 (leaving p anywhere) when the input reaches lim before the number ends, when more than 9 bytes would be needed, or when the last byte is 0 although it is not the first byte (not the shortest form).

`lz_chk_size(t int) int` is the size in bytes of the xz check field of type t: 0 for type 0, 4 for types 1 to 3, 8 for 4 to 6, 16 for 7 to 9, 32 for 10 to 12, 64 for 13 to 15, and -1 for anything above 15.

`lz_check(t int, d const byte[:], p int, data const byte[:]) bool` verifies the check field of type t that sits at offset p of d against data: type 1 compares the 4-byte little-endian number with `pack::pk_crc32(data, 0)`, type 4 compares `lz_le64(d, p)` with `lz_crc64(data, 0)`, type 10 compares the 32 bytes with `crypto::sha256(data)`; every other type (no check, or one not implemented) is true.

`var lz_recu int[>..]` and `var lz_recs int[>..]` hold the unpadded size and the uncompressed size of each block of the stream being read. `var lz_fid int[>..]` and `var lz_fpr int[>..]` hold, for the filter list of the current block, each filter's id and its first property byte (-1 when it has none).

`lz_xz_block(d const byte[:], p int, t int, out byte[>..]&, upad int&, usz int&, endp int&) bool` decodes the block whose header starts at offset p of d (the byte there is not 0) in a stream whose check type is t, appends the block's data to out and sets upad (the unpadded size: header size plus compressed size plus check size), usz (the uncompressed size) and endp (the offset after the check field). hs is (the byte at p plus 1) times 4; fail when p plus hs passes the length of d. Fail with the warning "xz: header CRC mismatch" through `lz_warn` when `pack::pk_crc32` of the bytes p up to p plus hs minus 4 differs from the 4-byte little-endian number at p plus hs minus 4. flags is the byte at p plus 1; nf is (flags and 3) plus 1; fail when any of the bits 0x3C of flags is set. q is p plus 2. When bit 0x40 of flags is set the compressed size is a varint read with `lz_varint` from q up to p plus hs minus 4 (fail when negative), otherwise -1; when bit 0x80 is set the header's uncompressed size is read the same way, otherwise -1. Then clear `lz_fid` and `lz_fpr` and for each of the nf filters read id as a varint and psz as a varint (fail when either is negative or q plus psz passes p plus hs minus 4); push id onto `lz_fid` and push onto `lz_fpr` the property value, which is -1 when psz is 0, the byte at q when psz is 1, the 4-byte little-endian number at q when psz is 4 (any other psz is only allowed for ids that fail anyway); then q grows by psz. The filter list must look like this: only the last filter has id 0x21 (LZMA2), with psz 1 and a property byte of 40 or less; every earlier one has id 3 (Delta) with psz 1, or id 4 (BCJ x86) with psz 0 or 4; any other filter id fails with the warning "xz: unsupported filter" through `lz_warn`, and any other violation fails. The bytes from q up to p plus hs minus 4 must all be 0. When a size is known from the header and out's length plus it passes `lz_cap`, fail through `lz_over()`. `lz_dict` becomes `lz_dsize` of the last filter's property value. bstart is out's length and ds is p plus hs. Call `lz_lzma2_run(d, ds, false, out, e)` and fail when it fails; fail when the compressed size is known and differs from e minus ds; usz is out's length minus bstart, and fail when the header's uncompressed size is known and differs from it. Then undo the other filters in the reverse of their listed order (from the second-to-last one down to the first one): Delta calls `lz_delta(out, bstart, property value plus 1)`; BCJ x86 calls `lz_x86(out, bstart, ip)` where ip is the property value, or 0 when it is -1. Block padding: pad is the number of bytes (0 to 3) that makes e minus p a multiple of 4; those bytes must exist in d and all be 0; then e grows by pad. cs is `lz_chk_size(t)`; fail when cs is negative or e plus cs passes the length of d. When `lz_check(t, d, e, out[bstart..])` is false fail with the warning "xz: CRC mismatch" through `lz_warn`. endp is e plus cs and upad is hs plus (the compressed size, that is e minus pad minus ds) plus cs.

`lz_xz_stream(d const byte[:], p int, out byte[>..]&, endp int&) bool` decodes one whole stream that starts at offset p of d and appends its blocks to out; endp is the offset after its footer. Fail when the 12-byte stream header at p is missing or is not valid: the first six bytes are the xz magic, the byte at p plus 6 is 0, the byte at p plus 7 is below 16 (t is that byte; the check type), and `pack::pk_crc32` of the two bytes at p plus 6 equals the 4-byte little-endian number at p plus 8 (otherwise warn "xz: header CRC mismatch"). Clear `lz_recu` and `lz_recs`; q is p plus 12. Repeat: fail when q is at the end of d; when the byte at q is 0 leave the loop (the index begins); otherwise call `lz_xz_block(d, q, t, out, up, us, e)`, fail when it fails, push up onto `lz_recu` and us onto `lz_recs` and set q to e. The index starts at i0 equal to q: the byte 0 there, then n is a varint that must equal the number of blocks read; for each block a varint unpadded size and a varint uncompressed size follow (the reads stop at the end of d) and must equal the recorded ones; then zero bytes (they must be 0) up to the next multiple of 4 counted from i0, then the 4-byte CRC-32 that must equal `pack::pk_crc32` of the bytes from i0 up to there; after it q is the offset after the CRC and the index size is q minus i0. The 12-byte footer at q must fit: its CRC-32 (4 bytes little-endian at q) equals `pack::pk_crc32` of the 6 bytes at q plus 4; the backward size (4 bytes little-endian at q plus 4) plus 1 times 4 equals the index size; the two flag bytes at q plus 8 equal those of the header; the last two bytes are 0x59 0x5A. endp is q plus 12. Return true.

`lz_unxz(d const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out, sets `lz_capped` and `lz_said` to false and decodes .xz data made of one or more streams one after another, appending all their blocks to out. When d is not `lz_is_xz` add the warning "xz: not an xz file" and return false. Decode a stream with `lz_xz_stream` at p (starting at 0); when it fails clear out, add the warning "xz: corrupt or truncated data" unless `lz_capped` is set or `lz_said` is true, and return false. After each stream skip the zero bytes that follow it (stream padding): their count must be a multiple of 4 (otherwise fail as above). When nothing remains the data is done; when at least 12 bytes remain and they satisfy `lz_is_xz` they are another stream (a damaged one makes the whole call fail); otherwise add the warning "xz: trailing bytes ignored" and stop. Return true after at least one stream.

`lz_is_lzma(d const byte[:]) bool` is true when d looks like the legacy .lzma format: at least 18 bytes, the properties byte at 0 below 225, the dictionary size (4 bytes little-endian at 1) equal to 4294967295, or to 2 to the power n for some n from 0 to 31, or to 3 times 2 to the power n for some n from 0 to 30, the uncompressed size (8 bytes at 5) either all 0xFF bytes or less than 274877906944, and the byte at 13 equal to 0.

`lz_unlzma(d const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out, sets `lz_capped` and `lz_said` to false and decodes a legacy .lzma file: the 13-byte header (properties byte, 4-byte dictionary size, 8-byte uncompressed size where all 0xFF bytes mean unknown) followed by the range coded data. When d is not `lz_is_lzma` add the warning "lzma: not an lzma file" and return false. size is the uncompressed size, or -1 when unknown (then the stream must end with the end marker); call `lz_lzma_dec(d[13..], d[0..5], size, out)`; anything after the end of the data is ignored. When it fails clear out and return false.
